ath11k: shrink the data-path rings to fit a 256 MB board

ath11k sizes its DP rings and buffer pools from compile-time constants in
dp.h that are identical for every chip and tuned for IPQ8074/IPQ6018 class
APs with 512 MB - 1 GB of RAM.  On the 256 MB IPQ5018 in cmcc,pz-l8 that
costs about 79 MB, measured: MemAvailable drops from 87 MB to 7.7 MB the
moment both radios finish QMI init, leaving nothing for the rest of the
system and the board reboots under load.

The cost is the buffer pools rather than the descriptors - every RXDMA ring
entry is backed by a DP_RX_BUFFER_SIZE skb, so 4096 entries is roughly
11 MB per radio, and IPQ5018 sets .rxdma1_enable = true so the monitor
rings are allocated too.

The vendor driver avoids this with a memory profile ath11k has no
equivalent of: its ring sizes are runtime INI values, and
lib/wifi/qcawificfg80211.sh picks them from the DT marker MP_256 / MP_512,
writing dp_nss_comp_ring_size 0x2000 for a 256 MB board against 0x8000 for
the unmarked default - and 0x8000 is exactly ath11k's hardcoded
DP_TX_COMP_RING_SIZE.

Values follow openwrt PR #21495 (kmod-ath11k-smallbuffers), which reported
about 67 MB freed and was not merged because the maintainer does not want
to carry 256 MB boards.  DP_RXDMA_MONITOR_DESC_RING_SIZE is added here: it
is another 4096-entry monitor ring that the PR leaves alone.

The monitor rings only serve radiotap capture, which an AP does not do.
MON_STATUS still feeds rate and RSSI reporting so it is only halved, and
DP_RXDMA_BUF_RING_SIZE, which bounds in-flight Rx, is the one to raise
again if the measured headroom allows.


Two of these are raised again, above upstream in one case, because the NSS
wifili offload needs the depth: the firmware reaps the Tx completion and Rx
release rings itself and the vendor sizes them 8192 and 4096.  Measured with
them at the shrunk values, the NSS core trapped under sustained traffic; at
these values it does not.  The monitor rings stay small - an AP does not do
radiotap capture - and DP_RXDMA_BUF_RING_SIZE stays at 1024, which is what
bounds the resident Rx buffer pool and therefore the memory this patch exists
to save.

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/dp.h
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/dp.h
@@ -206,24 +206,24 @@
 #define DP_WBM_RELEASE_RING_SIZE	64
 #define DP_TCL_DATA_RING_SIZE		512
 #define DP_TCL_DATA_RING_SIZE_WCN6750	2048
-#define DP_TX_COMP_RING_SIZE		32768
+#define DP_TX_COMP_RING_SIZE	8192
 #define DP_TX_IDR_SIZE			DP_TX_COMP_RING_SIZE
 #define DP_TCL_CMD_RING_SIZE		32
 #define DP_TCL_STATUS_RING_SIZE		32
 #define DP_REO_DST_RING_MAX		4
 #define DP_REO_DST_RING_SIZE		2048
 #define DP_REO_REINJECT_RING_SIZE	32
-#define DP_RX_RELEASE_RING_SIZE		1024
+#define DP_RX_RELEASE_RING_SIZE		4096
 #define DP_REO_EXCEPTION_RING_SIZE	128
 #define DP_REO_CMD_RING_SIZE		256
 #define DP_REO_STATUS_RING_SIZE		2048
-#define DP_RXDMA_BUF_RING_SIZE		4096
+#define DP_RXDMA_BUF_RING_SIZE	1024
 #define DP_RXDMA_REFILL_RING_SIZE	2048
 #define DP_RXDMA_ERR_DST_RING_SIZE	1024
-#define DP_RXDMA_MON_STATUS_RING_SIZE	1024
-#define DP_RXDMA_MONITOR_BUF_RING_SIZE	4096
-#define DP_RXDMA_MONITOR_DST_RING_SIZE	2048
-#define DP_RXDMA_MONITOR_DESC_RING_SIZE	4096
+#define DP_RXDMA_MON_STATUS_RING_SIZE	512
+#define DP_RXDMA_MONITOR_BUF_RING_SIZE	128
+#define DP_RXDMA_MONITOR_DST_RING_SIZE	128
+#define DP_RXDMA_MONITOR_DESC_RING_SIZE	128
 
 #define DP_RX_RELEASE_RING_NUM	3
 
